  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe center of expansive surface high pressure drifts across central \nLower Mi to dominate conditions today. It has already delivered a \ncool and dry air mass to the region as evidenced by temperature \nobservations dropping into the upper 40s outside of Detroit and away \nfrom the shorelines. Also impressive is temperature guidance that \nstruggles to break 70 this afternoon despite full sun, although we \nare getting closer to the equinox. \n\nHigh clouds are on the increase this evening which signal a quick \ntransition to the next low pressure system. It is projected to move \nout of the Rockies and along the Canadian border today and occlude \nacross NW Ontario tonight. This track sets up the low level jet and \nplume of moisture transport from the central Plains into the \nnorthern Great Lakes which then settles into central Lower Mi by \nsunrise Tuesday. Behavior of regional and hi-res deterministic \nmodels reflects a tendency for erosion of the precipitation pattern \nby dry air lingering out ahead of the system. There is also some \nbreakup caused by the moisture axis outrunning the upper jet and mid \nlevel short wave, both of which are positioned well to the north \nacross northern Ontario and the northern Great Lakes. Mostly chance \nor scattered POP guidance in the NBM looks good in this regard until \nflaring up later Tuesday afternoon. Daytime heating is projected to \nboost convection by then, mainly across metro Detroit to the Ohio \nborder, as the cold front crosses the area into Tuesday evening. The \nSPC Day 2 outlook grazes Lenawee and Monroe counties in a Marginal \nRisk for severe intensity. \n\nThe surface cold front makes it south of the IN/OH border by \nWednesday morning where it stalls and wavers across central IN/OH \nthrough Wednesday night and Thursday. This keeps a chance of showers \nin the forecast for SE Mi, mainly south of I-69 as elevated portions \nof the frontal zone are activated by southward expanding upper level \njet. The 00Z models maintain a consensus on a more coherent mid \nlevel short wave and surface low pressure reflection along the \nstationary front late Wednesday night and Thursday. ML/AI runs of \nthe GFS and ECMWF help narrow down areas closer to the Ohio border \nfor the highest POPs and QPF, and in general agreement with the WPC \nMarginal outlook for excessive rainfall.  \n\nWe get a break in showers/storms by Friday in the subsidence wake of \nthe passing short wave. Building surface high pressure drives the \nfrontal zone well down into the Ohio valley where it takes most of \nSaturday to begin a northward return. Global/extended range models \nbring the next low pressure system back into the upper Midwest \nSaturday and through Lower Mi Sunday.",
